Add a Windows console backend

A second definition of every function of the platform layer, under
_WIN32, drives the Windows console through the Win32 console API:

  - tb_init opens CONIN$ and CONOUT$; tb_init_rwfd takes C runtime
    descriptors, finds out with GetConsoleMode which of them are
    consoles and treats the others (pipes, files) as byte streams.
  - Raw mode is the console in virtual terminal mode: the console
    encodes keys as the escape sequences of an xterm
    (ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_INPUT), interprets the escape sequences
    termbox2 writes (ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_PROCESSING), reports
    window size changes, and uses UTF-8 in both directions. Quick edit
    mode is switched off. Modes and code pages are restored at
    shutdown. Mouse reporting (ENABLE_MOUSE_INPUT) follows the
    TB_INPUT_MOUSE bit of tb_set_input_mode, because a pseudo console
    asks its terminal to report the mouse as soon as a program asks the
    console.
  - Waiting uses WaitForSingleObject on the console input handle;
    records that mean nothing to termbox2 (key releases, focus and
    menu events) are dropped and the wait goes on. Keys are read with
    ReadConsoleInputW and encoded as UTF-8, surrogate pairs included;
    the text a key release carries after an Alt+numpad entry is kept.
    Mouse records of a legacy console window become the SGR mouse
    reports (mode 1006 with motion, as in mode 1003) termbox2 parses
    anyway; Windows Terminal sends those bytes itself. A window size
    change becomes a TB_EVENT_RESIZE; one that arrives while a caller
    waits for input only is kept for the next tb_peek_event.
  - The size is the console window's (srWindow), not the screen
    buffer's, which a legacy console keeps taller for its scrollback.
  - Pipes are polled with PeekNamedPipe every few milliseconds; files
    are read directly; an end of file appends nothing, as read(2)
    returning 0 does.
  - Output goes through WriteFile in chunks, since the console takes
    at most some tens of kilobytes at once.
  - Without a terminfo database, the built-in capabilities of
    xterm-256color are used unless TERM is set. tb_strerror formats
    Win32 error codes (tb_last_errno) with FormatMessage.

TB_OPT_LIBC_WCHAR is refused on Windows, whose C library has no
wcwidth(3). The POSIX build is unchanged by this patch.

Earlier attempts at Windows support upstream: pull requests 58 and 66
(a compat layer of macros, closed), 123 (a shell script that edits the
header, open), 129 (a multi-platform restructuring with WebAssembly
support, open).
